   149	## §3. Per-gap defense families
   150	
   151	### Shared infrastructure (precedes ⑤ and ⑥) — `v2-AlertBridge-Framework`
   152	
   153	**Why**: Q6 operator decision = file AlertRule tickets BEFORE 31.G ships, but design them so the future AM bridge connection is a zero-surprise swap. Both v2-⑤-AlertRule and v2-⑥-AlertRule (and any future v2-* alert) consume this framework, so it's a single shared piece, not duplicated per family.
   154	
   155	**Design forward-compat checklist** (the 7 AM-integration pitfalls we plan around now):
   156	
   157	| Pitfall | Mitigation baked into framework |
   158	|---|---|
   159	| Label/annotation semantic drift between rule and AM | Required-label schema fixed in v2-AlertBridge-Framework: `severity`, `area`, `family`, `defense_dimension`; required annotations: `summary`, `description`, `runbook_url`, `remediation_hint` |
   160	| Severity-vocabulary mismatch | Fixed enum `severity ∈ {page, warn, info}` (P1/P2/P3); AM routing reads this directly |
   161	| Dedupe key collision across rules | Contract: `dedupe_key = sha256(alertname + family + sorted_critical_labels)`; rules MUST declare which labels are critical |
   162	| Resolved-notification surprise | Policy fixed: `severity=page` emits P3 recovery on resolution; `warn`/`info` silently resolve |
   163	| Time-series cardinality explosion | Each label capped at ≤10 distinct values; framework validator rejects rules that can produce more |
   164	| Receiver routing inconsistency | Routing decision in framework config (severity → channel map), not in individual rules |
   165	| Group_by / group_wait fragmentation | Standard grouping: `group_by: [severity, area]`, `group_wait: 30s`, `group_interval: 5m`, `repeat_interval: 4h` — same as 31.G 9a will use |
