     1	# Multi-Agent Coordination
     2	
     3	> **Status**: Active operating contract for Claude (Opus) + Codex (GPT)
     4	> + human operator. Last updated 2026-05-02.
     5	> **Companions**: `CLAUDE.md` (Claude's L1 rules), `AGENTS.md` (Codex's
     6	> L1 rules), `docs/operations/runner-strategy.md` (architecture
     7	> rationale).
     8	
     9	---
    10	
    11	## What this doc is
    12	
    13	This is the **single source of truth** for "who does what" on
    14	OmniSight-Productizer when more than one agent (or operator) is active
    15	on the repository simultaneously. It exists because:
    16	
    17	  * Multiple Claude runners on the same git branch is safe (proven
    18	    empirically — same model, consistent judgement, file-level
    19	    non-overlap when sections differ).
    20	  * Adding Codex breaks the "consistent judgement" assumption →
    21	    needs explicit boundaries.
    22	  * Without those boundaries, throughput goes negative (time spent
    23	    cleaning up exceeds time saved by parallelism).
    24	
    25	If you are a new agent / operator joining this repo, read this doc
    26	first, then `runner-strategy.md`, then your model-specific rules
    27	(`CLAUDE.md` if you're Claude, `AGENTS.md` if you're Codex).
    28	
    29	---
    30	
    31	## Section ownership (default routing)
    32	
    33	| TODO area | Default owner | Reason |
    34	|---|---|---|
    35	| `backend/agents/*` infra | Claude | Load-bearing internal runtime; consistency critical |
    36	| `backend/auth.py` / `audit.py` / multi-tenant boundary | Claude | Security domain, design judgement |
    37	| `KS.*` (encryption / multi-tenant) | Claude | Compliance + design |
    38	| `BP.A` (templates) / `BP.B` (Guild reorg) / `BP.C` (T-shirt gateway) / `BP.F` (model mapping) / `BP.H` (penalty) | Claude | Cross-subsystem integration |
    39	| `BP.A2A` / `BP.Q` (RAG) | Claude | New architectural pieces |
    40	| `W11`-`W16` (web sandbox / clone / preview / orchestrator) | Claude | Multi-subsystem; Codex would burn on max_tokens |
    41	| `HD.*` (high-density PCB / SI) | Claude | Domain-specific judgement |
    42	| `WP.*` (Wave-1 platform plumbing) | Claude | Cross-cutting infra |
    43	| `BP.D` (compliance matrices) | Claude | Auxiliary disclaimer + legal review |
    44	| `BP.K` (frontend 6 components) | Mixed | Architectural changes → Claude; UI text/styling → Codex |
    45	| **`FS.*` (full-stack adapter scaffolds)** | **Codex** | Pattern-replicated across adapters |
    46	| **`SC.*` (security scanning integrations)** | **Codex** | Wrapping existing tools (CodeQL/Semgrep/etc.) |
    47	| **`BP.D.7` (10+ audit skills markdown)** | **Codex** | Pattern-heavy markdown, GPT structures well |
    48	| **`BP.W3.1` (D3-D29 27 skill packs rework)** | **Codex** | High-volume pattern replication |
    49	| **`BP.G` (TDD dual-patchset)** | **Codex** | Mechanical Gerrit hooks |
    50	| **`BP.J.2` (post-merge git hook for self_healing_docs)** | **Codex** | Single-file glue, ~50 LOC |
    51	| **Documentation updates** (operator runbooks, READMEs) | **Codex** | Markdown is GPT's strong domain |
    52	| **Tests for already-implemented code** (`*.5 Tests` items in `FS.*` / `SC.*`) | **Codex** | Mirroring existing test classes |
    53	| **ESLint 113 findings cleanup** (`B9`) | **Codex** | High-volume mechanical fixes |
    54	
    55	**Default rule when ambiguous**: ownership goes to Claude. Better to
    56	slow Claude down than have Codex make judgement calls in
    57	unfamiliar territory.
    58	
    59	The human operator can override any assignment for specific tasks.
    60	
    61	---
    62	
    63	## Tier rules (per-task safety classification)
    64	
    65	Every task an agent picks up is **Tier A** or **Tier B**.
    66	
    67	### Tier A — direct commit to current branch
    68	
    69	Use when **all** of these hold:
    70	
    71	  * Output is structurally constrained (you're following a pattern
    72	    that exists at least 2x in the codebase already)
    73	  * Single file or 1-2 closely-related files
    74	  * If your output is wrong, recovery is `git revert <commit>` and
    75	    nothing else breaks
    76	  * Task description fits in 1 line plus an example
    77	
    78	Examples:
    79	  * "Add SES adapter mirroring Resend adapter shape"
    80	  * "Add 5 more test cases to TestApiChangeReport class following
    81	    existing pattern"
    82	  * "Update operator runbook to reflect new env knob X"
    83	
    84	### Tier B — commit to isolated branch, review before merge
    85	
    86	Use when **any** of these hold:
    87	
    88	  * Multi-file change spanning logically separate concerns
    89	  * New module / new public API surface
    90	  * Anything touching auth / encryption / audit / multi-tenant
    91	  * Task description requires interpretation of intent
    92	  * Ownership is "Mixed" in the table above
    93	  * **You're not sure which Tier**
    94	
    95	Worktree workflow for Tier B (Codex):
    96	```bash
    97	# Codex operates from this worktree (set up by human, see below)
    98	cd ../OmniSight-codex-worktree
    99	git switch codex-work          # already on this branch by default
   100	# ... do work, commit, etc ...
   101	# When done, human or Claude reviews, then:
   102	cd /path/to/main/checkout
   103	git merge codex-work           # operator merges manually
   104	```
   105	
   106	### Decision rule
   107	
   108	If you are uncertain, **assume Tier B**. Tier B has higher coordination
   109	cost but lower failure cost. Tier A failures land directly in the main
   110	branch — costlier to clean up.
   111	
   112	---
   113	
   114	## Worktree layout (local-only, no remote push)
   115	
   116	The repo has 1 main checkout at `/home/user/work/sora/OmniSight-Productizer`
   117	(branch: `main`) plus 1 codex-dedicated worktree.
   118	
   119	Setup (one-time, done by Claude on 2026-05-02):
   120	```bash
   121	git -C /home/user/work/sora/OmniSight-Productizer branch codex-work main
   122	git -C /home/user/work/sora/OmniSight-Productizer worktree add \
   123	    /home/user/work/sora/OmniSight-codex-worktree codex-work
   124	```
   125	
   126	After setup, two physical directories share the same `.git`:
   127	  * `/home/user/work/sora/OmniSight-Productizer/`        → branch `main`
   128	  * `/home/user/work/sora/OmniSight-codex-worktree/`     → branch `codex-work`
   129	
   130	**Routing convention**:
   131	  * Claude runners: always work from the main checkout on `main`.
   132	  * Codex Tier A tasks: from main checkout on `main` (rare — only for
   133	    pattern-replication tasks the human pre-approved as Tier A).
   134	  * Codex Tier B tasks (default): from worktree on `codex-work`.
   135	
   136	Codex's runner script picks the working directory automatically based
   137	on the task's tier (see `auto-runner-codex.py`).
   138	
   139	---
   140	
   141	## Same-branch parallel-runner safety contract
   142	
   143	When multiple runners (Claude × N + maybe Codex Tier A) work on the
   144	**same** branch simultaneously, the following invariants must hold —
   145	otherwise concurrent commits / TODO writes corrupt state:
   146	
   147	1. **Section non-overlap**: each runner has a distinct
   148	   `OMNISIGHT_RUNNER_FILTER` — no two runners pick from the same
   149	   section. Reading the section table above, each section has at most
   150	   one default owner; even when human overrides, no two concurrent
   151	   runners share a section.
   152	
   153	2. **File non-overlap**: cross-section file overlap (e.g., two
   154	   sections both touching `backend/main.py`) means the section
   155	   ownership default is wrong — escalate to human, do not work in
   156	   parallel on overlapping files.
   157	
   158	3. **TODO marker disjoint**: Claude marks `[x][C]` / `[!][C]` /
   159	   `[~][C]` / `[O][C]`; Codex marks `[x][G]` / `[!][G]` / `[~][G]` /
   160	   `[O][G]`. Each runner only modifies markers with its own letter.
   161	   Reading another's marker is fine; modifying is not.
   162	
   163	   **Tier B special**: in Tier B (codex worktree), the **codex runner
   164	   itself** writes the `[G]` marker on main TODO, NOT codex inside
   165	   the worktree. This is because the worktree has its own TODO.md
   166	   snapshot — codex modifying it never reaches main and causes
   167	   infinite-loop dispatch. The runner pre-reserves with `- [~][G]`
   168	   before dispatch and flips to `[x]` / `[!]` after. Codex's prompt
   169	   (AGENTS.md Rule 6) tells it not to touch TODO.md in Tier B.
   170	   Discovered + fixed 2026-05-03 after FS.1.1 looped on auto-runner-
   171	   codex.py's first real run.
   172	
   173	4. **HANDOFF.md heading prefix**: Claude entries start with `##
   174	   [Claude/Opus]`; Codex entries start with `## [Codex/GPT-5.5]`.
   175	   Append-only, never edit other agent's entries.
   176	
   177	5. **Commit messages MUST be atomic and authored**: every commit
   178	   carries Co-Authored-By trailers identifying the agent
   179	   (`Claude Opus 4.7` / `GPT-5.5 (codex-cli)`) plus env user + global
   180	   user. `git log --grep "GPT-5.5"` and `git log --grep "Claude Opus"`
   181	   should cleanly separate authorship for audits.
   182	
   183	If any of these invariants is violated, **stop the runner**, clean up
   184	manually, fix the routing, then resume.
   185	
   186	---
   187	
   188	## What if the agents disagree
   189	
   190	Concrete scenarios + resolutions:
   191	
   192	| Scenario | Resolution |
   193	|---|---|
   194	| Codex's Tier A commit lands in main with a style that Claude finds inconsistent | Human (or Claude) opens a discussion in HANDOFF; if needed, Claude can adjust style in a follow-up commit |
   195	| Codex marks `[x][G]` but Claude's later session finds the work incomplete | Claude flips it to `[!][G]` (NOT `[!][C]` — preserves authorship) and writes a HANDOFF entry explaining the gap |
   196	| Both agents independently start on the same task | The one running it second sees the section already has a marker change → stops, writes HANDOFF `[blocked]: race detected with <other agent>` |
   197	| Codex needs to modify a Claude-owned file (e.g., `backend/agents/anthropic_native_client.py`) | Codex stops, writes `[codex-blocked]: this task seems to need changes to Claude-owned area; suggesting <X>`. Human / Claude takes over. |
   198	| Claude wants to refactor a Codex-authored module | Claude does it on `main`. The refactor commit references the original Codex commit hash so audit trail is clear. No special protocol — Claude has architectural authority. |
   199	
   200	The human operator is the final arbiter for any disagreement that
   201	isn't resolvable via the rules above.
   202	
   203	---
   204	
   205	## What this doc does NOT cover
   206	
   207	  * Remote git operations (push / pull / PR review on GitHub) — you
   208	    may not push to remote per current operator policy.
   209	  * Multi-tenant / multi-customer concerns — out of scope until
   210	    OmniSight ships as a product.
   211	  * Inter-agent direct communication (Claude → Codex via A2A) —
   212	    BP.A2A epic owns this; not implemented yet.
   213	  * Memory sync between agents — each agent has its own auto-memory
   214	    (`~/.claude/projects/...` for Claude, `~/.codex/...` for Codex),
   215	    they don't share. If we need cross-agent learnings, we collect
   216	    them manually into `docs/agents/learnings.md` (deferred until
   217	    needed).
   218	
   219	---
   220	
   221	## Quick reference card
   222	
   223	If you're an agent and not sure what to do RIGHT NOW:
   224	
   225	```
   226	Q1: What's my model?
   227	    Claude → CLAUDE.md is your rules; you can work directly on main.
   228	    Codex  → AGENTS.md is your rules; default to codex-work branch.
   229	
   230	Q2: Is the task on the section ownership table?
   231	    Yes → that owner does it.
   232	    No  → ask human, or default to Claude.
   233	
   234	Q3: Is this Tier A (pattern-mirroring, 1-2 files) or Tier B (anything else)?
   235	    Tier A → commit to current branch.
   236	    Tier B → switch to codex-work (Codex) or stay on main (Claude).
   237	
   238	Q4: How do I mark TODO when done?
   239	    Claude → - [x][C]  (or [!][C] / [~][C] / [O][C])
   240	    Codex  → - [x][G]  (or [!][G] / [~][G] / [O][G])

