## §4. Per-question answers (Q-A through Q-G)

### Q-A. Completeness of the 5 gap families (⑤⑥⑦⑧⑨)

⑤ shipped-not-deployed: partial. The spec covers runtime version surfacing, manifest bake, audit script, timer, alert, and E2E (`docs/sprint-s12/sprint-s12g-A-v2-runtime-defense-contract-spec.md:216-232`). It does not close the retrospective'"'s auto-pull / auto-redeploy consumer-side gap ("'`docs/retrospectives/2026-05-14-host-reboot-image-db-drift.md:106-114`). Adjacent failure modes missing: stale non-`:latest` tags, GHCR inaccessible during audit, digest changed but compose `pull_policy: missing` still reuses local image, and DB schema ahead after manual migration. D4 is declared N/A (`220`) but operational recovery is still manual; that is hand-wavy unless a separate deploy path owns it.

⑥ image-vs-DB drift: partial. It covers Option C startup behavior, gauge/routine scrape, readyz remediation, alert, rescue CLI, and drills (`docs/sprint-s12/sprint-s12g-A-v2-runtime-defense-contract-spec.md:184-213`). The retro lists causes such as manual prod DB alembic, cross-branch merge, and rollback residue (`docs/retrospectives/2026-05-14-host-reboot-image-db-drift.md:162-170`), but the spec does not define advisory locking, multi-head resolution, failed partial migration recovery, downgrade eligibility, or data-loss rules. D4 is deterministic only for the happy forward path; D5 is still hand-wavy because `v2-⑥-RescueCLI` combines backup, downgrade, manual upgrade, override, and audit without policy detail (`209`).

⑦ allowlist single-source: partial. It addresses the root drift class by making all five middleware consume `PUBLIC_PATH_ALLOWLIST` (`docs/sprint-s12/sprint-s12g-A-v2-runtime-defense-contract-spec.md:240-257`). It does not fully cover the retro'"'s uncertainty about auth strict-mode enforcement layers and mount ordering ("'`docs/retrospectives/2026-05-14-host-reboot-image-db-drift.md:177-183`). Adjacent missing modes: reverse-proxy health paths, `/api/v1/health` vs `/health`, docs/OpenAPI exceptions, WebSocket/auth bypasses, and new middleware not using `is_public()`. D4 is N/A, but D2 remediation should include a failing current-state reproduction before refactor.

⑧ shutdown contract: partial. It covers scripts, systemd ExecStop, compose grace periods, PG safety check, docs, drill, soak (`docs/sprint-s12/sprint-s12g-A-v2-runtime-defense-contract-spec.md:260-276`). The retro'"'s host-level forced WSL limit is not solved by Productizer-internal changes alone ("'`docs/retrospectives/2026-05-14-host-reboot-image-db-drift.md:17-25`, `184-192`), and Windows hooks are explicitly out of scope (`docs/sprint-s12/sprint-s12g-A-v2-runtime-defense-contract-spec.md:49-53`). Adjacent missing modes: unattended Windows Update, laptop power loss, Docker daemon stop order, Postgres checkpoint timeout, and post-boot forced-shutdown detection. D4 is better than other families but still lacks deterministic evidence for Windows-initiated shutdown.

⑨ optional auxiliary: partial. It covers app-level optionality: probe, gauge, feature flag, fallback-chain exclusion, decision, and integration (`docs/sprint-s12/sprint-s12g-A-v2-runtime-defense-contract-spec.md:278-298`). It does not fully cover the retro'"'s daemon-resource hygiene problem from a crashlooping sister container ("'`docs/retrospectives/2026-05-14-host-reboot-image-db-drift.md:194-199`). Adjacent missing modes: health endpoint absent, network partition to auxiliary, flapping threshold, stale capability inventory, and auxiliary stack consuming disk/log/CPU while app correctly excludes it. D4/D5 are declared N/A (`288`), but host cleanup still needs a deterministic decision / timer if the retro gap is to be closed.

### Q-B. Atomic decomposition compliance (per SOP §1-§3)

Because the required SOP document is missing from this checkout, this is a best-effort audit against the prompt'"'s stated rules and the visible G.A-v1 precedent requirement.

Likely tier:S after expansion: "'`v2-A1` schema only, `v2-A2` six forbidden combinations if fixtures are bounded, `v2-A4` golden fixture update, `v2-⑤-1bc` endpoint, `v2-⑤-Dockerfile-Manifest` if manifest script already exists or is split, `v2-⑦-1bc` single helper module, `v2-⑦-FixHealth` after the decision is locked, `v2-⑧-2b` compose grace periods, and `v2-⑨-2bc` fallback-chain builder if probe/flag already exists.

Likely must split or tier:M/L: `v2-A3` because it spans many validators and cross-field rules (`docs/sprint-s12/sprint-s12g-A-v2-runtime-defense-contract-spec.md:56-113`, `306-314`); `v2-⑥-RescueCLI` because it spans CLI, backup, downgrade/manual upgrade, L2 fingerprint, override, audit, and operator window (`209`); `v2-⑨-1bc` because probe + Prometheus gauge + in-process feature flag + flapping behavior is three surfaces (`295`); `v2-⑧-2a` because systemd refactor and operator prepare-only live unit work is not a bounded code pickup (`271`); `v2-⑤-2bc-Timer` because systemd timer + evidence write + Discord post crosses ops, docs/audit, and integration (`230`); `v2-AlertBridge-1bc` because adapter + cardinality + dedupe + tests is likely more than one bounded implementation (`176`); `v2-A9-SDValidator` because regex semantics + YAML parser + exceptions + soft-fail + override hook is large (`314`).

Tier:M+ integration check: fail. `v2-⑦-Integration`, `v2-⑨-Integration`, `v2-A7-Integration`, and `v2-AlertBridge-AMMigrationTest` are all tier:S even though they are explicitly integration / migration smoke tests (`178`, `257`, `298`, `312`). `v2-⑤-Integration`, `v2-⑥-Integration`, and `v2-⑧-Integration` are already tier:L (`212`, `232`, `276`).

### Q-C. Boundaries declaration completeness

Boundary fields are absent across the overview tables (`docs/sprint-s12/sprint-s12g-A-v2-runtime-defense-contract-spec.md:171-179`, `199-213`, `222-232`, `248-257`, `265-276`, `290-298`, `304-314`). At least these tickets need explicit blocks:

- `v2-⑥-RescueCLI` (`209`): `mutex_with: [prod-deploy, alembic-upgrade, backup-restore]`; `destructive_op_classes: [db-downgrade, manual-schema-upgrade, backup-create]`; `scope_components: [backend, alembic, ops-cli, audit]`; `external_side_effect: network-production`; `runtime_capability: network-production-when-apply`; `evidence_class: operator-rehearsal + audit-log`; `tag_type: operator-window`. This crosses DB + ops + audit boundaries and should be tier:M/L.

- `v2-⑧-2a` (`271`): `mutex_with: [prod-compose-restart, windows-shutdown-drill]`; `destructive_op_classes: [service-stop, systemd-unit-change]`; `scope_components: [deploy, systemd, scripts]`; `external_side_effect: local-host-service`; `runtime_capability: production-lifecycle`; `evidence_class: systemd-analyze + dry-run + operator-window`; `tag_type: operator-prepare-only`. It likely touches `/etc/systemd` outside repo, so filing must specify whether the repo change is a unit template or live host edit.

- `v2-⑤-2bc-Timer` (`230`): `mutex_with: [deployment-audit-run, prod-compose-recreate]`; `destructive_op_classes: []` unless it posts to external systems; `scope_components: [scripts, docs/audit, systemd, discord-integration]`; `external_side_effect: network-production + discord-post`; `runtime_capability: scheduled-production-audit`; `evidence_class: JSON evidence + notification transcript`; `tag_type: operator-prepare-only`. It crosses script, docs/audit, ops, and external notification.

- `v2-AlertBridge-1bc` (`176`): `scope_components: [backend/alerting, metrics, email]`; `external_side_effect: email-send` for warn/page; `runtime_capability: alert-routing`; `evidence_class: unit + adapter fake`; `destructive_op_classes: []`. Without this, email side effects can leak in tests.

- `v2-⑦-2bc` (`254`): `scope_components: [backend/auth_baseline.py, backend/main.py, backend/auth.py, backend/middleware_allowlist.py]`; `external_side_effect: auth-policy-change`; `runtime_capability: production-auth`; `evidence_class: auth contract tests`. This crosses a Claude-owned auth/security area per coordination defaults (`coordination.md:31-57`) and should not be codex tier:S without explicit operator override.

Likely cross-area failures: `v2-⑦-2bc` touches auth/security; `v2-⑤-2bc-Timer` touches scripts + docs/audit + Discord integration; `v2-⑥-RescueCLI` touches backend/alembic + ops CLI + audit; `v2-⑧-2a` touches repo plus live systemd unit; `v2-AlertBridge-*` touches backend alerting + deploy/prometheus + notification channels.

### Q-D. SOP §2 file-path → area-label audit

The spec does not list file-touch sets. Five inferred examples:

1. `v2-⑤-1bc` (`docs/sprint-s12/sprint-s12g-A-v2-runtime-defense-contract-spec.md:227`): likely paths `backend/main.py`, `backend/routers/version.py` or existing router package, `backend/tests/test_version_endpoint.py`, maybe `Dockerfile.backend` only via manifest consumer. Required area labels: `area:backend`, `area:deployment` if manifest fields are runtime-deploy metadata. Suggested `required_paths: [backend/routers/**, backend/main.py, backend/tests/**]`.

2. `v2-⑤-Dockerfile-Manifest` (`228`): likely paths `Dockerfile.backend`, `scripts/bake-image-manifest.sh`, `backend/alembic/versions/**` read-only reference, tests for script. Required labels: `area:devops`, `area:deployment`, maybe `area:backend-build`. Suggested `required_paths: [Dockerfile.backend, scripts/bake-image-manifest.sh, backend/alembic/versions/**]`.

3. `v2-⑥-2bc` (`206`): likely paths `Dockerfile.backend` or backend startup module, alembic env/config, `scripts/bootstrap_prod.sh`, tests. Required labels: `area:backend`, `area:db`, `area:deployment`. It conflicts with class codex/S because prod DB migration startup is high-risk and likely operator-window. Suggested `required_paths: [backend/**, backend/alembic/**, Dockerfile.backend, scripts/bootstrap_prod.sh, backend/tests/**]`.

4. `v2-⑦-2bc` (`254`): likely paths `backend/auth_baseline.py`, `backend/main.py`, `backend/auth.py`, `backend/middleware_allowlist.py`, backend auth tests. Required labels: `area:auth`, `area:backend`, `area:security`. Conflict: class codex despite auth/security ownership defaulting to Claude (`coordination.md:31-57`). Suggested `required_paths: [backend/auth_baseline.py, backend/auth.py, backend/main.py, backend/middleware_allowlist.py, backend/tests/test_*auth*]`.

5. `v2-⑧-2b` (`272`): likely path `docker-compose.prod.yml`, compose tests/lints, maybe docs. Required labels: `area:devops`, `area:deployment`, `area:ops`. Suggested `required_paths: [docker-compose.prod.yml, docs/ops/**, scripts/**]`.

Other conflicts: `v2-⑤-2bc-Timer` likely needs `area:integration` because it posts to Discord (`230`), while class is `claude + operator-prepare-only`; okay but should be explicit. `v2-AlertBridge-2bc` touches CI and deploy/prometheus (`177`) but is class codex; likely okay only if path-limited to linter and tests. `v2-A9-SDValidator` touches governance plugin + ADR override hook (`314`), so required paths may cross governance/runner-owned areas.

Suggested field: every ticket should add:

```yaml
required_paths:
  allow:
    - backend/...
    - scripts/...
  deny:
    - test_assets/**
    - coordination.md
  required_area_labels:
    - area:backend
    - area:deployment
  filing_check: reject_if_changed_path_not_covered
```

### Q-E. Cross-phase dependency correctness

31.G-LibsGate-external: partially reversed/over-broad. Spec says it blocks v2-⑥-AlertRule and v2-⑤-AlertRule because Prometheus + AM stack are needed (`docs/sprint-s12/sprint-s12g-A-v2-runtime-defense-contract-spec.md:352-354`), but the spec also says alert rules can file before 31.G with stdout/email v0 delivery and AM migration later (`151-180`, `389-390`). Better shape: AlertRule contract/spec can proceed before 31.G; AM webhook migration test blocks on 31.G concrete receiver schema.

31.C-Integration-external: plausible. `v2-⑤-2bc-Timer` posts to Discord (`230`), so Discord routing dependency (`354`) is correct if the timer really posts externally. If v0 stdout/email is enough, Discord should be moved to integration only.

31.H-StabilityCheckpoint-external: correct direction as written. Spec says it is NOT blocking and ⑧ should inform 31.H BackupDRDrill (`355`). That matches the retrospective that 31.H restore drill is bottom-of-funnel and shutdown contract is top-of-funnel (`docs/retrospectives/2026-05-14-host-reboot-image-db-drift.md:184-192`).

31.I-2bc: correct direction. Spec says 31.I consumes v2-⑥ healthcheck-validator and waits on us (`356`). The architecture note also frames defense_contract as a Layer 1 primitive consumed downstream (`docs/architecture/2026-05-14-runner-pivot-and-three-layer-architecture.md:238-245`).

Missing dependencies:
- v2-⑥ likely depends on 31.H canary-runner design if Option C names canary-runner canonical path (`docs/sprint-s12/sprint-s12g-A-v2-runtime-defense-contract-spec.md:203-206`; retro canary coverage at `docs/retrospectives/2026-05-14-host-reboot-image-db-drift.md:142-145`, `162-165`). If 31.H is not shipped, G.A-v2 must either own startup migration independently or state canary-runner is conceptual only.
- v2-⑤ likely depends on 31.E CI Wave 2 image manifest / publish contract because it compares running `/version` to latest GHCR digest (`docs/sprint-s12/sprint-s12g-A-v2-runtime-defense-contract-spec.md:226-232`; retro `OP-1035` build-side at `docs/retrospectives/2026-05-14-host-reboot-image-db-drift.md:33-35`, `143`).
- v2-⑦ likely depends on 31.B or other runner/governance plugin work if `PUBLIC_PATH_ALLOWLIST` must become a filing-time policy hook. The current spec treats it as independent after v2-A1 (`docs/sprint-s12/sprint-s12g-A-v2-runtime-defense-contract-spec.md:343`) but it touches auth policy and CI integration.

Sequencing risk: G.A-v2 -> G.B -> 31.B is not tight enough. §6 says G.B unblocked after all families and stability checkpoint (`347-350`), but §7 says 31.A-31.K v2 tickets can treat defense_contract as optional for four weeks after checkpoint (`391`). If one ship slips, G.B can consume partially hardened validators while phase tickets are still optional, causing a mixed regime. Add explicit "G.B may file only with validator version X and optional-mode flag Y" gates.

### Q-F. Hidden-assumption surface

Other implicit assumptions and how to test them:

1. Assumption: backend startup can safely run `alembic upgrade head` in production with two backend replicas racing. Test: concurrent startup integration with advisory lock contention; assert exactly one upgrader, other waits/fails deterministically.

2. Assumption: `image_alembic_head` can be computed reliably from baked manifest and not from stale filesystem state. Test: build image from branch with multiple heads / merge migration and compare manifest value to `alembic heads` inside container.

3. Assumption: exit code 78 propagates through Docker Compose/systemd in a way operators and alerts can distinguish from generic crash. Test: compose/systemd harness asserts unit status, logs, restart policy behavior, and alert payload for exit 78.

4. Assumption: all five auth middleware can share `is_public(path)` without changing path normalization, prefix matching, trailing slash, root path, or mounted router behavior. Test: table-driven tests for raw path, `scope['"'path']"'`, `/api/v1` prefix, slash variants, docs/static routes, and mounted routers.

5. Assumption: daily audit evidence can be written under `docs/audit/...` from production without dirtying the repo or colliding with git ownership. Test: run timer under production service user and verify write permissions, rotation, and no committed secrets / transient data.

6. Assumption: alert label cardinality can be statically capped at <=10 values (`docs/sprint-s12/sprint-s12g-A-v2-runtime-defense-contract-spec.md:155-165`). Test: run rule-linter against generated labels from real instances/images/services and fail if cardinality exceeds the declared cap.

7. Assumption: `AI_CORE_AVAILABLE` as an in-process flag is coherent across multiple backend workers / replicas. Test: two-process backend fixture where one probe changes state; assert both replicas update capability inventory within one probe cycle or move flag to shared store.

8. Assumption: optional auxiliary unavailability is safe to hide from users except capability inventory. Test: force ollama down during active Local LLM request; assert request is routed to a valid provider or returns a non-fatal, explicit provider-unavailable response.

9. Assumption: WSL2/systemd shutdown path used in drills matches Windows Update forced shutdown. Test: operator rehearsal using actual Windows shutdown/update simulation, not only `kill -9 systemd` (`docs/sprint-s12/sprint-s12g-A-v2-runtime-defense-contract-spec.md:275`).

10. Assumption: G.A-v2 bootstrap overrides can be audited after the validator ships and corrected without invalidating already-filed tickets. Test: file a deliberately bad bootstrap ticket in staging JIRA, run v2-A8 self-audit, verify override resolution workflow and remediation path.

### Q-G. SD-1 self-compliance (dog-food check)

Fail / soft-fail but important. The spec defines SD-1 at `docs/sprint-s12/sprint-s12g-A-v2-runtime-defense-contract-spec.md:115-147`, including trigger phrases and required `evidence_grep_artifact`. The spec itself contains novelty phrases or close equivalents:

- "5 new required fields" (`43`)
- "6 new combinations" (`113`, `307`)
- "new" defense dimensions for ⑥/⑤/⑦/⑧/⑨ (`188`, `220`, `238`, `263`, `288`)
- "v2-A8-SelfAudit (NEW)" (`313`, `389`)
- "v2-AlertBridge-Framework (NEW shared plumbing)" (`390`)

No corresponding evidence block exists; the only evidence block is the schema example itself (`123-133`). Some of these are internal change descriptors rather than historical novelty claims, so the validator should probably exclude local schema/ticket-change terms. But as written, SD-1 says ticket descriptions containing novelty phrases require evidence, and the v2-* ticket table descriptions contain them. That is a self-compliance failure.

The architecture memo also contains the companion novelty correction and then asserts what is "actually new" (`docs/architecture/2026-05-14-runner-pivot-and-three-layer-architecture.md:135-148`) without an `evidence_grep_artifact`. If the G.A-v2 validator covers architecture memos (`docs/sprint-s12/sprint-s12g-A-v2-runtime-defense-contract-spec.md:136-140`), that document will warn/fail too.

Because the prompt says the 4-week pilot is soft-fail, I would not block solely on SD-1. But combined with the absent corpus and missing boundaries, this remains a P0 filing-quality issue.

